# Trust chain for comunidad.comprasdominicana.gob.do.
#
# The portal serves only its leaf certificate and omits the intermediates.
# Browsers paper over that by fetching them from the AIA URL; curl does not, so
# every scrape fails with 'unable to get local issuer certificate'. This file
# supplies what the portal omits, and PortalScraperService passes it as the
# verify bundle.
#
# Nothing new is trusted. Every anchor below is already in the OS store; the
# file only makes the missing intermediates reachable.
#
# TWO chains are present on purpose. Pinning to one CA is what broke this:
# the portal was on Let's Encrypt until 2026-10-27, switched to Sectigo EV
# that day, and from 2026-11-02 every request failed verification for three
# days because this bundle only knew Let's Encrypt. Keeping the previous CA
# costs nothing and covers a rollback.
#
#   leaf -> Sectigo Public Server Authentication CA EV R36
#        -> Sectigo Public Server Authentication Root R46      (current)
#   leaf -> YR2 -> Root YR (cross-signed by X1) -> ISRG Root X1 (until 2026-10-27)
#
# If the portal switches CA again, nothing needs doing: on the first trust
# failure PortalTrustStore fetches the new issuer from the leaf's AIA URL,
# keeps it only if it verifies against the OS store, and stores it under
# storage/app/portal-trust/ — the scrape retries and carries on. The learned
# issuer can be copied in here afterwards so a fresh deploy starts with it:
#
#   # read the issuer and its AIA URL off the live leaf
#   echo | openssl s_client -connect comunidad.comprasdominicana.gob.do:443 \
#     -servername comunidad.comprasdominicana.gob.do 2>/dev/null \
#     | openssl x509 -noout -issuer -ext authorityInfoAccess
#   curl -o int.der <CA Issuers URI> && openssl x509 -inform DER -in int.der -out int.pem
#   # confirm the intermediate's own root is already trusted before adding it
#   openssl verify -CApath /etc/ssl/certs int.pem
#   cat int.pem /etc/ssl/certs/<That_Root>.pem >> resources/certs/comprasdominicana-ca.pem
#
